[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn-frontpage", :item-id "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50305218", :title "Show HN: Sketch Stadium – watch your agent play Pictionary", :link "https://sketch.wakeshift.ai/games/q5ucru", :published-at #inst "2026-10-30T15:23:11.000-00:00", :content "tl;dr: Agents can play Pictionary. You can connect your own agent, or just watch a game in progress here: https://sketch.wakeshift.ai/games/q5ucru Hi folks! I initially built this as a proof-of-concept for sending push notifications to AI agents. Watching the agents play Pictionary together has been compelling, so I thought I'd ship it as a standalone demonstration. In Sketch Stadium, your agent subscribes to game events. Agents take turns either drawing or guessing. Easy clues are 1 point, medium are 2, and hard are 3. It's entertaining to watch and some of the drawings are impressive (and others are mind-bogglingly bad). This is the real opportunity for the model labs who have been pelican-maxxing to shine. The first version gave agents the ability to draw lines 10 at a time so that it would be a little bit more like real Pictionary. However, it turns out agents are _really_ bad at drawing that way. So instead I let them use a subset of SVG to draw and that's worked much better, even for the lower-cost models. You don't need to create an account to connect your agent, just add the MCP and authorize. But if you don't want to do that, just watch the game linked above. As a bit more background, agents often start processes that take an indeterminate amount of time to complete (e.g. CI, waiting for approval in a separate system, sending an email and waiting for a reply). If an agent needs that process to complete before it can proceed, it can: * poll, * ask the user to tell it when it's done, * use a bespoke built-in integration, or * use a proprietary capability (e.g. Claude Code monitors) Today, there's no easy, consistent way to wake up agents through a webhook or push notification. I dealt with this firsthand with Clawvisor (authorization for agents) where I needed agents to recognize when the operator had clicked an out-of-band approve button. The best-supported way to achieve that at the time was long-polling. This project is basically a tech demo for Wakeshift, which is a cross-harness, simple SDK for providing push notifications for agents. It attaches to your MCP to allow consumers of your MCP to subscribe to real-time events, and uses whatever available hacks per harness to wake an agent when dormant. For most harnesses, Wakeshift requires a hook to be installed for the best experience. It will upgrade automatically as harnesses start adopting MCP events. I'd love any feedback or ideas for other products or games that you'd want to see built on this capability. Or MCP servers that you use today that you think would benefit from push notifications. Comments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50305218 Points: 15 # Comments: 6"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or * use a proprietary capability (e.g. Claude Code monitors) Today, there's no easy, consistent...", :matched-terms ["claude"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn-frontpage", :item-id "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50309479", :title "Show HN: Automatic deduction of FlashAttention kernel from ~25 lines of SQL", :link "https://github.com/xqlsystems/xarray-sql/blob/6debe2495dcb4e8fac20ab97df5b89baae2a31a6/benchmarks/ml/01_attention.py", :published-at #inst "2026-10-30T20:09:47.000-00:00", :content "In this demo, we automatically deduce a FlashAttention kernel with ~25 lines of SQL, which just expresses vanilla attention. This is possible due to three portable SQL extensions that we’re building: - xarray-sql: a library that efficiently represents petabyte scale array datasets in the cloud as tables (relations). - ddx: a database extension that adds automatic differentiation in the style of JAX to standard SQL, assuming the above array data model. - einfold: a modular query planner for fast tensor contractions in vanilla SQL, again, assuming the above array data model. My hope is that the relational model and algebra provide fresh perspectives on how we might do accelerator programming. Here, our thesis is that separating the logical layers in a tensor program from the physical layers is both more correct and more scalable. If the Bitter Lesson is a central dogma of machine learning, then why not formalize it with relational algebra? If this interests you and you’d like to tinker with us at the intersection of database theory and ML systems, join us on Discord at https://xql.systems (link at the bottom). Cheers. Comments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50309479 Points: 11 # Comments: 1"}, :excerpts [{:text "... If the Bitter Lesson is a central dogma of machine learning, then why not formalize it with relational...", :matched-terms ["machine learning"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://metaredux.com/posts/2026/10/30/neorepl-0-4", :title "neorepl 0.4: Tests, Inspector, Tracing and Agents", :link "https://metaredux.com/posts/2026/10/30/neorepl-0-4.html", :published-at #inst "2026-10-30T12:30:00.000-00:00", :content "neorepl 0.4 came out last week, and since I never got around to writing about 0.2 and 0.3, it feels like a good moment to look back at what happened since the original announcement. Three releases in two weeks, and neorepl now has most of what I listed under “What’s next” back then - a test runner, a full-screen inspector and stacktrace browser, an MCP server and a skill for coding agents - plus a few things that weren’t on the list at all, like tracing and peeking into your coding agent’s REPL session. Running tests ,test in the REPL, or neorepl test in your shell, runs the tests in the namespaces you name, the tests you name or, without either, the whole project’s. Failures come with their file and line, and errors with a stack trace that stops at the test that hit them, instead of wandering off into clojure.test’s guts. ,retest (neorepl retest) reruns only what didn’t pass, which is exactly what you want while you’re fixing things. As usual, the server does the heavy lifting. cider-nrepl runs the tests when it’s around, and so does babashka’s nREPL server, which bundles CIDER’s test ops these days. Without either, neorepl falls back to plain clojure.test, as long as you tell it which namespaces to test. And in a ClojureScript session the same commands run your ClojureScript tests. $ neorepl test\n$ neorepl test my.app-test my.app.db-test\n$ neorepl test --format json my.app-test/start-test\n Stack traces and the inspector ,stacktrace shows the last error full-screen. Pick a frame, press Enter, and its source opens in your $EDITOR at that line (as a copy, for code in a jar), and when you quit the editor you’re back where you were. By default you see only the frames that matter, and a brings back the rest - the REPL’s own machinery and the repeated frames of the same function. With cider-nrepl, i inspects the cause you’re looking at and d its ex-data. After a test run with errors, ,stacktrace shows those, each under the test it came from. (You can see that at the end of the GIF above.) ,inspect puts cider-nrepl’s inspector (babashka has one too) in full-screen: The arrow keys (or j and k) move around, Enter digs into a value and Backspace goes back up, [ and ] page through big collections, v shows an object’s fields, P toggles pretty printing and g refreshes values that change, like an atom’s. The handiest one is d, which defines whatever you’re looking at as a var. Dig five levels deep into some big API response, press d, give it a name, and you can play with that piece in the REPL right away. neorepl inspect does the same one step at a time, for scripts and agents, since the inspector keeps its state in the session: $ neorepl inspect '(System/getProperties)'\n$ neorepl inspect --push 3\n$ neorepl inspect --def props\n Tracing ,trace traces a function, or every function in a namespace, and from then on each call shows its arguments and what it returned, nested in the calls it’s part of. It’s often the quickest way to see what some code is actually doing, and there are no printlns to forget to remove afterwards. Calls on other threads, like an HTTP handler’s or a future’s, show up above the prompt too. ,trace without arguments lists what’s traced and ,untrace stops tracing. Scripts and agents get neorepl trace, with the calls showing up in the output of the neorepl eval that made them. Tracing needs cider-nrepl on the server. (Fun fact: tracing a function that the tracing itself calls, like clojure.core/map, used to take the whole server down. neorepl refuses to trace those, and as of orchard 0.48 the server no longer falls over on them anyway.) Coding agents 0.3 was all about coding agents. The CLI was usable by agents from day one, but now they get a lot more help: neorepl mcp serves neorepl’s features as MCP tools over stdio, for agents that would rather call tools than run commands. With Claude Code it’s a one-liner: $ claude mcp add neorepl -- neorepl mcp\n The tools are eval, load, doc, source, where, apropos, macroexpand, reload, test, retest, pst, interrupt, check and ports. That’s fewer than the CLI’s commands on purpose: an agent pays for every tool’s description in its context on every turn, whether it uses the tool or not. Evaluations get cut at 10,000 characters and interrupted after 60 seconds by default, so a stray (range) or an infinite loop can’t eat the agent’s context or its session. A skill teaches agents the workflow I’d like them to follow: after editing a file, check its parens, load it, run its tests, and read the stack trace when something fails. It costs nothing until the agent actually works on Clojure code. doc/agents.md explains how to set it up with Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, Cursor and VS Code. neorepl check --fix repairs unbalanced delimiters the way parinfer’s indent mode would, trusting the indentation over the parens. Mismatched parens are still the most common way for an agent to break a Clojure file, and now fixing them doesn’t take another trip through the model: $ neorepl check src/demo/orders.clj\nsrc/demo/orders.clj:3:1: Unclosed (\n$ neorepl check --fix src/demo/orders.clj\nsrc/demo/orders.clj:3:1: Unclosed (, repaired from the indentation\n The CLI and the MCP tools share named sessions, the one called agent unless told otherwise, which brings me to my favourite addition in 0.4. Looking over your agent’s shoulder ,sessions lists the server’s named sessions, and ,sessions agent steps into the agent’s, with its name in the prompt. Everything the agent left behind is right there: its namespace, its *1, its *e. So when an agent tells you that a test fails with a NullPointerException, you can look at the exception yourself instead of asking it to paste the stack trace. ,sessions - takes you back to your own session. The lookups take --session too, so neorepl doc --session agent foo looks up foo the way that session sees it, in its namespace and its language, ClojureScript included. The REPL, round two 0.4 also spent a lot of time on the REPL itself: ,ns switches namespaces, loading the one you name if it isn’t loaded yet, and lists the project’s namespaces when you don’t name one. C-x C-s shows the source of the symbol at the cursor and C-x C-a the vars whose names contain it, next to the C-x C-d for docs that was there from the start. All three come from rebel-readline. ,doc and the arglists that show up as you type cover Java classes, methods and fields too, with their Javadoc as text (that one needs cider-nrepl). ,edit and the stacktrace browser open definitions that live in jars, instead of telling you they can’t. Ctrl-C got a lot more reliable. An interrupt sent right after an evaluation could get lost when the server hadn’t started running it yet, the forms after an interrupted one ran anyway, and whatever an interrupted evaluation printed later could show up in the next one’s output. All fixed. neorepl sends the forms you enter to the server one at a time now, which is a big part of the interrupt fixes, and as a bonus piggieback evaluates all of them, not only the first. On Linux, every evaluation used to wait 40ms on the server’s socket, courtesy of TCP’s delayed ACKs. neorepl turns those off now, on the JVM and on babashka 1.13.227 or newer, and evaluating a file with 100 forms went from 4.5 seconds to about 150ms. Lots of error messages got friendlier: a build tool that isn’t installed, a server that dies while starting, loading a file with unbalanced parens, a named session the server lost when it restarted, and so on. So much for keeping neorepl small, by the way - the code has about doubled since 0.1. In my defense, it does a lot more than twice as much. Making the neighbours better, round two Just like last time, building neorepl turned up problems elsewhere, and a good part of these two weeks went into fixing them at the source: I filed a batch of issues for babashka’s nREPL server: test runs that hung or killed the session, :project? being ignored, a session that never answered again after its client disconnected, a couple of JLine classes neorepl’s line editor needed. Michiel fixed every single one of them within days, and they’re all in babashka 1.13.227. Thanks, Michiel! cider-nrepl 0.64 and orchard 0.48 came out this week with fixes for the problems I ran into: what tests print now comes back with the test results (it used to go to the connection of the session’s previous message, which for a CLI is long gone), the test op no longer goes silent for a namespace that isn’t loaded or runs every test when the one you asked for doesn’t exist, ClojureScript tests run on shadow-cljs, a traced call that throws shows up as such, and Javadoc no longer comes with stray spaces in it. CIDER gets all of that too, which is my favourite kind of side effect. The next neorepl release will use cider-nrepl 0.64 when it starts a server. In nREPL, the fix for an interrupted evaluation’s last response going to the wrong connection is merged, and so is TCP_NODELAY on the server’s sockets, which takes 100 evaluations with nREPL’s own client from 4.4 seconds to 90ms on Linux. Both will be in the next nREPL release. The piggieback improvements I mentioned last time are a bit behind schedule. A lot of ClojureScript REPLs run on top of piggieback, so a mistake there has a pretty big blast radius, and evaluating every form of a message is something we never found a good way to do in the past. I’d rather take my time with those than rush them out. neorepl isn’t waiting on them, at least, since it sends the forms one at a time now. What’s next 0.5 is about more runtimes and better navigation: integration tests against real servers in CI: cider-nrepl and babashka first, then shadow-cljs and piggieback with Node nbb and Basilisp in CI, with their gaps documented cross-references (,refs), on top of cider-nrepl’s fn-refs and fn-deps a full-screen namespace browser a couple of ideas borrowed from clojure-cli.repl: evaluating the form at the cursor, and the last evaluation’s outcome and time in the prompt indenting continuation lines, and a way to pick the vi keymap Better Windows support, the one item from the 0.1 list that’s still open, is planned for 0.6, together with easier ways to install neorepl. Try it $ bbin install io.github.nrepl/neorepl\n$ cd my-project\n$ neorepl\n The CHANGELOG has everything I skipped here, and the README has the details. Please keep the issues and ideas coming! Keep hacking!"}, :excerpts [{:text "neorepl 0.4: Tests, Inspector, Tracing and Agents", :matched-terms ["repl"], :source :title} {:text "neorepl 0.4 came out last week, and since I never got...", :matched-terms ["repl"], :source :content} {:text "... Three releases in two weeks, and neorepl now has most of what I listed under “What’s next”...", :matched-terms ["repl"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-10-30T21:51:33.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-10-30T21:51:33.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://blog.michielborkent.nl/oss-updates-sep-oct-2026.html", :title "OSS updates September and October 2026", :link "https://blog.michielborkent.nl/oss-updates-sep-oct-2026.html", :published-at #inst "2026-10-30T23:59:59.000-00:00", :content "In this post I&aposll give updates about open source I worked on during September and October 2026. To see previous OSS updates, go here. Sponsors I&aposd like to thank all the sponsors and contributors who make this work possible. Without you, the projects below would not be as mature or would not exist or be maintained at all! So a sincere thank you to everyone who contributes to the sustainability of these projects. Current top tier sponsors: Clojurists Together Roam Research Nextjournal Nubank Open the details section for more info about sponsoring. Sponsor info If you want to ensure that the projects I work on are sustainably maintained, you can sponsor this work in the following ways. If you work for a company that uses my OSS, please ask your employer, that would be even better. Thank you! GitHub Sponsors The Babashka or Clj-kondo OpenCollective Ko-fi Patreon Clojurists Together Intro A lot happened again in the last two months! One major highlight was my visit to the Clojure Conj together with Rahul Dé, where we organized a Babashka workshop. It was great seeing lots of familiar and new faces over there. To give people at the workshop the best experience possible, lots of improvements went into babashka prior to the Conj... workshop driven development I guess. The major milestones I focused on during the last 6-8 months were: Automatic command line help and completions in babashka tasks via babashka.cli Resolving dependencies without a JVM (by vendoring tools.deps and rewriting the maven part) Babashka FFI: a foreign function library to call C-ABI functions from libraries Better nREPL support: support popular nREPL middleware like CIDER&aposs test middleware, the CIDER inspector, etc TUI support via JLine All of this combined made for a great workshop, where we developed a stateless password manager that used cryptography either by shelling out or by FFI. This project combined the most-used libraries in babashka, like babashka.fs, babashka.process, babashka.cli, babashka.ffi, etc. so even if you didn&apost end up using this password manager (most people probably didn&apost) you certainly were exposed to what you would typically use in a bb script or project. Rahul and I will give this workshop at EuroClojure 2027 as well, so in case you missed it, you&aposll get one more chance! Highlights Here are some personal favorite highlights from the last two months. Squint now has source-map support was adopted by Hyper, a Datastar-centric web framework, to produce JS server side. Also see this blog post has a new playground design thanks to @paintparty (#1045) Antq, a tool to update outdated deps, now runs with bb! You can now run build.clj in babashka with tools.build (which comes bundled with bb) and get --help and shell completions for your build tasks. Sean Corfield has been adopting this in projects he maintains. See for example: next.jdbc, HoneySQL, logging4j2 and rephrase Babashka.ffi First Clojars release on 2026-09-18, for the JVM, native image, CLJS and nbb. Node.js 26.1+ through node:ffi JVM performance PRs by @Ramblurr (#36, #37) Adopted by sqlite4clj, which moved from coffi to babashka.ffi (#26, @Ramblurr) Clj-kondo Type system improvements: (when (and (string? x) (pos? n)) (inc x)) will now warn since (string? x) implies x is a string and (inc x) will crash on that input :unknown-map-key linter that warns when requesting keys from maps that provably aren&apost there: given (defn opts [] {:host \"localhost\"}), (:hots (opts)) will warn. Blog posts Blog posts that I published in this period: Babashka 1.13.222: the conj release (2026-09-14) https://blog.michielborkent.nl/babashka-1.13.222.html Tree shaking squint on the server with esbuild (2026-09-25) https://blog.michielborkent.nl/squint-tree-shaking-hyper-esbuild.html Updates Here are some highlights per project. See each project&aposs CHANGELOG.md for the full list. Babashka: native, fast-starting Clojure interpreter for scripting. Resolve dependencies without a JVM by default. Set :deps-resolver :jvm in bb.edn or the add-deps map, or BABASHKA_DEPS_RESOLVER=jvm, to use Java as before. See the blog post Make clojure.tools.deps and its script namespaces, including clojure.tools.deps.script.make-classpath2, available to scripts Bundle clojure.tools.build.api, with patched install and javac for babashka. A tools.build dependency on the classpath takes precedence and runs from source Add bb repl --connect [addr] to connect to an nREPL server with completion, eldoc, documentation lookup and Ctrl-C interruption. Accepts host:port, a port or unix://path. Reads .nrepl-port by default. Bind the nREPL server to 127.0.0.1 by default instead of 0.0.0.0. Use bb --nrepl-server 0.0.0.0:1667 in Docker Bundle nREPL ops for the CIDER inspector, test runner (C-c C-t) and cider-version Add the nREPL interrupt op. Interruption is cooperative and does not stop tight loops Smaller binary: the bundled Clojure sources are stored gzipped, and jline&aposs class files are no longer embedded a second time as resources. Add the CIDER debugger to the nREPL server: #dbg, #break, C-u C-M-x and the step commands Add CIDER&aposs track-state to the nREPL server: font-lock of loaded vars and the box around instrumented functions Add babashka.deps.maven with find-versions, with-repository-credentials, active-proxy and model-repos Block the certificates of the eMudhra intermediate CA compromise (@stathissideris) The macOS amd64 binary requires macOS 11.0 or later Full changelog babashka.ffi: call C functions in shared libraries from Clojure. 0.1.1: First standalone release of babashka.ffi, available on Clojars for JVM Clojure, ClojureScript and nbb Call C libraries from JVM Clojure on JDK 25 or newer, or from applications compiled with GraalVM 25 or newer. ClojureScript, nbb and shadow-cljs can call C libraries on Node.js 26.1 or newer through node:ffi #63: callback accepts struct layouts as argument and return types JVM call performance by @Ramblurr (#36, #37) New examples: JavaScriptCore, pinball, SDL3, libusb and QuickJS babashka.esbuild: use esbuild from babashka and the JVM New project. Bundle and transform JavaScript, TypeScript, JSX and CSS through babashka.ffi, without Node.js Expose esbuild&aposs metafile and metafile analysis (#3, @lread) Used by cljdoc and hyper deps-deploy: deploy jars to Clojars and Maven Central from babashka New project. Takes the options of slipset/deps-deploy, with transfers through babashka.http-client GPG signing, local install, snapshots and several jars in one deploy Maven Central through the Publisher Portal SCI: Configurable Clojure/Script interpreter suitable for scripting Clojure 1.13 map destructuring as of clojure/clojure 33fb064d (1.13.0-alpha11): :req!, :excess and :missing directives, :defaults removed. Adds the selector macro to clojure.core. Speed up binding (about 4x on the JVM, 2x in native bb) and try without a catch (about 4x) ns-map is 6x faster and ns-refers 60x Fix #1094: a call to a multi-arity fn created a new fn for the called arity on every call, 25x slower than a single-arity call in CLJS CLJS: creating a closure inside a jitted fn is 4x to 9x faster, and creating a multi-arity fn compiles instead of escaping to the interpreter Fix instance? on a protocol returning true for a type the protocol is extended to, such as nil or Object, on the JVM Full changelog clj-kondo: static analyzer and linter for Clojure code that sparks joy. NEW linter :unknown-map-key: warn on a keyword lookup of a key not present in a map. E.g. (:hots (opts)) will warn when opts returns {:host \"localhost\"}. Catch up with Clojure 1.13.0-alpha9 destructuring Type checker: a type predicate narrows the type of a local in if-not, cond and and. E.g. (when (and (string? x) (pos? n)) (inc x)) will warn. Type checker: merge and merge-with require a map or nil as first map argument. E.g. (merge [1 2] [3 4]) will warn. #2774: :warn-on-reflection also warns on Java constructor calls. E.g. (Date. x) will warn. #3006, #3014, #3015, #3016: fixes for hooks generated with :clj-kondo/macroexpand-hook: referred macros, helper namespaces, requires without alias and nested hooks (@imrekoszo) #2945: support :order-by, :having and :timeout in Datalevin and Datahike queries #2998: analyze Java 25 class files (@skylightis666) Full changelog Squint: CLJS syntax to JS compiler #453: Add source maps for compiled files #594: support for await in doseq, with ^:await on a binding, (doseq [^:await x xs] ...), or on the binding vector for every binding (#1053, @shaunlebron) Add eduction, implements? and keyword-identical? Optimize compiled output for or, and and reify Optimize compiled output for core predicates such as even? and string? in if, and, or and not #1032: #html: escape attribute values from {:& m} (@mskyan) Playground: Implement draggable resizing of editor and compile panes (#1045, @paintparty) Playground: Support codemirror dark theme across editor, repl output, and compiled code instances (#1045, @paintparty) Full changelog neil: A CLI to add common aliases and features to deps.edn-based projects 0.3.71: neil requires babashka 1.13.229 or later #254: auto-generated CLI help and shell completions via babashka.cli. neil dep --help no longer throws #272: make babashka.neil/-main easier to call from code (@lread) #268: neil dep upgrade LIB upgrades only LIB instead of all deps #267: neil dep add, dep upgrade and dep versions find versions in the :mvn/repos of deps.edn. dep versions no longer lists snapshots babashka CLI: Turn Clojure functions into CLIs! #226: use metadata and docstrings from :fn and :exec-fn vars in babashka and ClojureDart dispatch: add :supplied to the opts map&aposs :org.babashka/cli metadata with the set of option keys given on the command line #233: Generated help no longer prints extra spaces when :prog is not specified (@lread) Full changelog nbb: Scripting in Clojure on Node.js using SCI Add babashka.ffi built-in library for calling C functions. Requires Node.js 26.1 or newer Bump SCI: faster multi-arity function dispatch, bumps edamame to 1.6.44 Edamame: configurable EDN and Clojure parser with location metadata and more Performance: speed up parsing plain decimal numbers on the JVM #155 Performance: build map literals with transients #159 Require Clojure 1.10 or newer #158 deps.clj: a faithful port of the Clojure CLI Bash script to Clojure Allow --version, --help, -Sdescribe and a cached -Spath to run without Java. Add *make-classpath-fn* to allow in-process classpath resolution. Catch up with Clojure CLI 1.12.6.1673 rewrite-edn: Utility lib on top of rewrite-clj #39: Support namespaced maps like #:a{:x 1} neil#239: Read and update maps with metadata like ^{:x 1} {:a 1} Fix get, assoc, update and dissoc when a map value equals the key http-client: HTTP client for Clojure and babashka #86: uri-with-query no longer decodes the existing query, fragment and user info when :query-params are used (@dylanpulver) #91: decode-body passes a nil body through, e.g. of a 407 to CONNECT fs: file system utility library for Clojure #284: non-recursive match and glob now return immediate child directories, not only files #269: match and glob no longer drop pattern text after a 2nd colon on Unix (@marksto) #279: minimum supported version of Clojure is now 1.11 (@lread) html: HTML generation library inspired by squint&aposs HTML tag Omit attributes whose value is nil, or false in html mode: [:input {:disabled false}] rendered disabled=\"false\", which disables the input. Matches hiccup (#19, @telekid) Render true and false as \"true\" and \"false\" for aria-*, data-*, contenteditable, draggable, focusable and spellcheck. Names match in any case, so :spellCheck works as well quickblog: lightweight static blog engine for Clojure and babashka Add :pre-process-markdown-fn and :post-process-html-fn options to transform each post&aposs Markdown and HTML (README) (@jmglov) gh-release-artifact: Upload artifacts to GitHub releases idempotently Throw when Github does not accept an upload. A failed upload no longer leaves a green build Upload the new bytes before touching the existing asset when replacing one Buzz: write a web application with the JVM or babashka only New observe, atom-source and buzz.source/Source API. :watch and invalidate! are gone (#20) raylib-bb-ffi: raylib bindings for babashka via babashka.ffi New experiment, ported with an LLM. Tests whether the examples of raylib-jlt and raylib-clj run in babashka and on the JVM through babashka.ffi cljbang: a Clojure-like language that runs as Emacs Lisp Add for (#12) and juxt (#9, @kpassapk) Fix #10: str/join with a separator and an empty coll returns \"\" Cherry: Experimental ClojureScript to ES6 module compiler Bump squint: don&apost crash the nREPL server on a SyntaxError from eval (#208) Cream: Clojure + GraalVM Crema native binary Compare load times, tooling and GraalVM build with babashka (#7) lein-clj-kondo and clj-kondo-bb: released alongside each clj-kondo release Contributions to third party projects: antq: antq runs on babashka (#316, #380) and is installable through bbin (#386) hyper: compile h/expr against a served squint core, tree-shaken with esbuild. See the blog post. Serve source maps for defc components behind :source-map? nextjournal/markdown: #71: render the link title in the default :link renderer clojure-mode: bind Option+S to unwrap on macOS (#62) Other projects These are some other projects I&aposm involved with, but little to no activity happened in the past two months. Click for more details quickdoc: Quick and minimal API doc generation for Clojure pod-babashka-go-sqlite3: A babashka pod for interacting with sqlite3 pod-babashka-gozxing: a babashka pod for QR code and barcode decoding/encoding unused-deps: Find unused deps in a clojure project pod-babashka-fswatcher: babashka filewatcher pod sci.nrepl: nREPL server for SCI projects that run in the browser babashka.nrepl-client sci.configs: A collection of ready-to-use SCI configs. rewrite-clj: Rewrite Clojure code and edn tools-deps-native and tools.bbuild: use tools.deps directly from babashka bbin: Install any Babashka script or project with one command graal-build-time: initialize Clojure classes at build time for GraalVM native-image qualify-methods: experimental tool to rewrite instance calls to use fully qualified methods (Clojure 1.12 only) tools: a set of bbin installable scripts babashka.json: babashka JSON library/adapter speculative squint-macros: a couple of macros that stand-in for applied-science/js-interop and promesa to make CLJS projects compatible with squint and/or cherry. http-kit: Simple, high-performance event-driven HTTP client+server for Clojure. jet: CLI to transform between JSON, EDN, YAML and Transit using Clojure lein2deps: leiningen to deps.edn converter cljs-showcase: Showcase CLJS libs using SCI babashka.book: Babashka manual pod-babashka-buddy: A pod around buddy core (Cryptographic API for Clojure). carve - Remove unused Clojure vars 4ever-clojure - Pure CLJS version of 4clojure, meant to run forever! pod-babashka-lanterna: Interact with clojure-lanterna from babashka clj2el: transpile Clojure to elisp deflet: make let-expressions REPL-friendly! deps.add-lib: Clojure 1.12&aposs add-lib feature for leiningen and/or other environments without a specific version of the clojure CLI process: Clojure library for shelling out / spawning subprocesses parmezan: fixes unbalanced or unexpected parens or other delimiters in Clojure files Reagami: A minimal zero-deps Reagent-like for Squint and CLJS Scittle: Execute Clojure(Script) directly from browser script tags via SCI Choq: a ~5 MB binary running the cherry compiler on embedded QuickJS squint-inline: write squint functions and inline expressions in a ClojureScript project graaljs-cherry: a native-image cherry REPL on GraalJS clj-kondo-browser: a static Clojure source browser built from clj-kondo analysis grasp: Grep Clojure code using clojure.spec regexes"}, :excerpts []}]